Ahmed arag spends his days in the  support man in Istanbul the 29 year old  hasn't been able to work since he fell  and endured his wrist two years ago  he needs surgery but can't afford it and  his landlord wants to increase the rent  Ahmed like many other Syrian refugees is  struggling as a Turkish economy awakens  I'm very frustrated I can't go to  another country I can't get treated here  and I can't return to Aleppo to see my  family I can't enjoy simple things in  life like other people my age here I'm a  stranger the worst thing is that I'm  young but can't work I have to sit at  home psychologically it's very upsetting  once hailed as a success story among  emerging economies trickier's inflation  exceeded 85 percent this year coupled  with Rising nationalism ahead of an  election in mid-2023 it has sparked the  backlash against Syrian refugees the  opposition has promised to send them  back most syrians we have approached in  Istanbul do not want to be interviewed  on camera because they're afraid of  being deported despite deteriorating  economic conditions and true care they  don't want to return home because  because they say a feature in Syria  doesn't hold much promise Walid ottuman  is an exception he spent eight years in  Turkey working all jobs that paid poorly  in 2020 he moved back to azalez to  realize his dream of going College even  though the diplomas are not  International recognized now while it  works for a local media organization my  friends told me not to return to Syria  and start over they said I wouldn't be  able to improve myself but here I am any  achievement in my country is better than  in another country this is more valuable  to me I hope all those syrians in Turkey  can return home to better conditions one  day Syria's opposition-held areas are  also affected by the inflation across  the border as they use the Turkish  currency we are trying to make basic  food items available but we make only a  small profit because of the changing  prices in a year the Turkish lira has  lost 40 percent of its value against the  US dollar and inflation is expected to  remain high in 2023 despite government  promises to reduce it leaving syrians  whether they live as refugees in turkey  or have returned home to opposition-held  areas with limited opportunities  Al Jazeera Istanbul
 

